# GUIDELINES AND TIPS
- Start by using the method and class search tools, with their vector databases, to retrieve the most relevant methods and classes for each step.
- Use code metadata tools to understand the codebase and determine method/class relevance.
- For each step, identify one to three candidate methods (either library or external methods), ordered by preference with the best candidate first. If the step likely uses an external method, indicate that using the external parameter.
- Refine the initial decomposition into the best step set for faithful execution. Merge, split, reorder, or remove steps as needed. If the code indicates more steps should be needed to execute a desired behavior, introduce and localize those steps in the same scenario format. Merge steps that can be executed as a single method call.
- Verify each candidate with tool evidence.
- Use `get_inherited_library_classes` to identify inherited library classes and consequent callable library methods on a class, if the application methods are not sufficient.
- Aim for each step to map cleanly to one concrete best method when possible.
- If a step cannot be localized and has no clear justification, remove it from the scenario. An example of a valid non-localized step to remain in the scenario, with a note explaining why it is not localized, is a step that instantiates a primitive variable.
- Avoid testing private methods, and watch for package mismatches. Running reachability checks before selecting a candidate is recommended.
- For each selected method, inspect its signature and supply argument bindings by listing each parameter name with its value. Values may be literals or ${...} placeholders referencing earlier returns.
- Do not rely solely on vector-index similarity when searching for relevant methods; also identify relevant classes and explore their reachable application methods, or use class metadata to retrieve inherited methods from external classes.
- Do not assume application behavior or code. Use tool evidence to verify relevant application methods when localizing each step.

# END CONDITIONS
Your task is complete when:
- You have sufficient understanding of the application code and tool evidence to localize all steps.
- Each step is either assigned one to three candidate methods in best-first order, removed, or explicitly marked as non-localized with notes.
- Each localized step has argument bindings and relevant, succinct comments justifying the decision.
OR you have run out of iterations.
To complete the task, call `finalize` with the fully localized scenario and specific comments on the localization process. Use your tool-call log to produce the best localized scenario, and use the comments to note any issues or key design considerations.